      "math_academy_id": "e-43",
      "problem": "Calculate  $\\lim_\\limits {x\\rightarrow -1} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} .$",
      "worked_solution": "We can sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^4}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14247879/assets/99f04a25115f084e40e9f8f9b06a6749532a2ba2320ee23d31af050a87957269.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}= \\infty.\n$$",
